4. All files you write must go to this directory: E:\agent_dev\multi-agent\experiments\costprobe\sb\tmp{"query": "TimeGPT Nixtla architecture parameters transformer time series foundation model", "follow_up_questions": null, "answer": null, "images": [], "results": [{"url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666827025001847", "title": "Benchmarking a time-series foundation model (TimeGPT) for real-world forecasting applications", "content": "Introduced by Nixtla in 2023, TimeGPT is the first foundation model for time-series forecasting. It leverages a transformer-based encoder–decoder architecture with positional encoding and multi-head attention (Vaswani et al., 2017), enabling zero-shot forecasting without task-specific training. Trained on more than 100 billion time-series data points (Garza et al., 2024) from domains including finance, healthcare, weather, demographics, and transport, TimeGPT is designed to generalize across [...] Foundation models for time series represent a promising shift in this landscape. Trained on large, heterogeneous time-series corpora, they aim to generalize across domains with minimal customization, reducing the need for task-specific feature engineering (Demir & Karaoğlan, 2024). TimeGPT is one such model: a transformer-based foundation model that offers zero-shot forecasting across multiple domains. Its simplicity of deployment makes it attractive for resource-constrained settings, including [...] Recent research has extended transformer-based architectures to create domain-general forecasting models that can operate across tasks without local retraining. Examples include TimeGPT (Garza et al., 2024), which employs large-scale pretraining over diverse temporal datasets; Chronos (Ansari et al., 2024), which tokenizes numeric values for probabilistic forecasting; and PatchTST (Nie et al., 2023), which applies patch-based self-attention to capture local temporal dependencies. These models", "score": 0.8808076, "raw_content": null, "id": "f54722-00"}, {"url": "https://github.com/Nixtla/nixtla", "title": "GitHub - Nixtla/nixtla: TimeGPT-1: production ready pre-trained Time Series Foundation Model for forecasting and anomaly detection. Generative pretrained transformer for time series trained on over 100B data points. It's capable of accurately predicting various domains such as retail, electricity, finance, and IoT with just a few lines of code 🚀. · GitHub", "content": "The architecture consists of an encoder-decoder structure with multiple layers, each with residual connections and layer normalization. Finally, a linear layer maps the decoder’s output to the forecasting window dimension. The general intuition is that attentionbased mechanisms are able to capture the diversity of past events and correctly extrapolate potential future distributions. [...] TimeGPT is user-friendly and low-code, enabling users to upload their time series data and either generate forecasts or detect anomalies with just a single line of code. As the only foundation model for time series analysis out of the box, TimeGPT can be integrated via our public APIs, through Azure Studio (coming soon), or deployed on your own infrastructure.\n\n## ⚙️ TimeGPT's Architecture [...] Self-attention, the revolutionary concept introduced by the paper “Attention is all you need“, is the basis of the this foundational model. The TimeGPT model is not based on any existing large language model(LLMs). It is independently trained on vast timeseries dataset as a large transformer model and is designed so as to minimize the forecasting error.", "score": 0.8399576, "raw_content": null, "id": "75c81f-01"}, {"url": "https://www.r-bloggers.com/2025/02/a-first-look-at-timegpt-using-nixtlar-2", "title": "A First Look at TimeGPT using nixtlar | R-bloggers", "content": "As described in Garza et al. (2021), TimeGPT is a Transformer-based time series model with self-attention mechanisms. The architecture comprises an encoder-decoder structure with multiple layers, each with residual connections and layer normalization. The encoder, a stack of multi-head self-attention layers followed by a feed-forward neural network, processes the input time series. The decoder, which is similar to the encoder, generates the forecast. The decoder includes an additional [...] This post is a first look at Nixtla’s TimeGPT generative, pre-trained transformer for time series forecasting using the nixtlar R package. As described in Garza et al. (2021), TimeGPT is a Transformer-based time series model with self-atten... [...] \" property=\"og:description\"> This post is a first look at Nixtla’s TimeGPT generative, pre-trained transformer for time series forecasting using the nixtlar R package. As described in Garza et al. (2021), TimeGPT is a Transformer-based time series model with self-atten...\n\n\" name=\"twitter:description\">\n\nR-bloggers\n\n# R-bloggers\n\n## R news and tutorials contributed by hundreds of R bloggers\n\n# A First Look at TimeGPT using nixtlar", "score": 0.8014549, "raw_content": null, "id": "156d65-02"}, {"url": "https://www.nixtla.io/docs/introduction/about_timegpt", "title": "About TimeGPT - TimeGPT Foundational model for time series forecasting and anomaly detection", "content": "TimeGPT is a production-ready generative pretrained transformer model specifically designed for time series forecasting. It accurately forecasts domains such as retail, electricity, finance, and IoT with minimal code. Below you’ll find a high-level overview of its features, architecture, and practical examples.\n\n  \n\n1\n\n1. Start Now\n\n## Activate Free Trial\n\n## Quickstart Guide\n\n2\n\n2. Choose Your Interface\n\nYou can access TimeGPT through: [...] TimeGPT’s architecture builds on the self-attention mechanism introduced in the original “Attention is All You Need” paper. Unlike typical large language models (LLMs), TimeGPT is independently trained on extensive time series datasets to minimize forecasting errors. [...] Irregular Timestamps: Process data with non-uniform timestamps directly, with no extra preprocessing.\n\nAnomaly Detection\n\nAnomaly Detection: Identify anomalies automatically, integrating external features for improved precision.\n\nGet started quickly with the Quickstart guide. Explore in-depth tutorials on TimeGPT capabilities and real-world applications.\n\n## ​ Architecture\n\nTimeGPT Architecture Overview\n\nTimeGPT Architecture Overview", "score": 0.7548612, "raw_content": null, "id": "4378af-03"}, {"url": "https://aihorizonforecast.substack.com/p/timegpt-the-first-foundation-model", "title": "TimeGPT: The First Foundation Model for Time Series", "content": "## TimeGPT: The First Foundation Model for Time Series\n\nIn the first cohort of the “Modern Forecasting in Practice” course by top forecasting experts Tim Januschowski and Jan Gasthaus, the Nixtla team also participated.\n\nDuring one of the sessions, as the conversation turned to the future of research in time series and modern large-scale architectures, Nixtla implied that public datasets were not large enough to test the potential of those larger architectures.\n\nTheir observation was spot-on. [...] 1. TimeGPT: Nixtla unveiled TimeGPT, the first foundation AI model for time-series forecasting.\n2. AutoGluon–TimeSeries: Amazon published a study on AutoGluon–TimeSeries, an open-source library that generates accurate point & quantile forecasts with just 3 lines of Python code.\n\nLet’s dive in!\n\nHorizon AI Forecast is a reader-supported newsletter, featuring occasional bonus posts for supporters. Your support through a paid subscription would be greatly appreciated.", "score": 0.74961954, "raw_content": null, "id": "bfc40b-04"}], "response_time": 1.42, "request_id": "b69cc012-57e3-461f-8f75-28afe5fcf46b"}{"query": "Chronos Amazon time series foundation model architecture T5 parameters", "follow_up_questions": null, "answer": null, "images": [], "results": [{"url": "https://huggingface.co/amazon/chronos-2", "title": "amazon/chronos-2 · Hugging Face", "content": "Chronos-2 is a 120M-parameter, encoder-only time series foundation model for zero-shot forecasting.\nIt supports univariate, multivariate, and covariate-informed tasks within a single architecture.\nInspired by the T5 encoder, Chronos-2 produces multi-step-ahead quantile forecasts and uses a group attention mechanism for efficient in-context learning across related series and covariates. [...] prediction_length=24, # Number of steps to forecast\nquantile_levels=[0.1, 0.5, 0.9], # Quantiles for probabilistic forecast\nid_column=\"id\", # Column identifying different time series\ntimestamp_column=\"timestamp\", # Column with datetime information\ntarget=\"target\", # Column(s) with time series values to predict\n)` [...] `from autogluon.cloud import TimeSeriesFoundationModel\nmodel = TimeSeriesFoundationModel(model_name=\"chronos-2\")\n# Batch prediction\nforecast_df = model.predict(df, prediction_length=24)\n# Deploy & invoke a real-time endpoint\nendpoint = model.deploy(instance_type=\"ml.g5.xlarge\")\nforecast_df = endpoint.predict(df, prediction_length=24)`\n\nFor more details (e.g. serverless endpoints, covariate-aware forecasting), see the full deployment guide.\n\n### 🚀 SageMaker JumpStart", "score": 0.86767644, "raw_content": null, "id": "ca10da-00"}, {"url": "https://github.com/amazon-science/chronos-forecasting", "title": "GitHub - amazon-science/chronos-forecasting: Chronos: Pretrained Models for Time Series Forecasting · GitHub", "content": "### Available Models\n\n| Model ID | Parameters |\n --- |\n| `amazon/chronos-2` | 120M |\n| `autogluon/chronos-2-synth` | 120M |\n| `autogluon/chronos-2-small` | 28M |\n| `amazon/chronos-bolt-tiny` | 9M |\n| `amazon/chronos-bolt-mini` | 21M |\n| `amazon/chronos-bolt-small` | 48M |\n| `amazon/chronos-bolt-base` | 205M |\n| `amazon/chronos-t5-tiny` | 8M |\n| `amazon/chronos-t5-mini` | 20M |\n| `amazon/chronos-t5-small` | 46M |\n| `amazon/chronos-t5-base` | 200M |\n| `amazon/chronos-t5-large` | 710M | [...] Chronos: The original Chronos family which is based on language model architectures. A time series is transformed into a sequence of tokens via scaling and quantization, and a language model is trained on these tokens using the cross-entropy loss. Once trained, probabilistic forecasts are obtained by sampling multiple future trajectories given the historical context. To learn more about Chronos, check out the publication. [...] = future_df prediction_length = 24 # Number of steps to forecast quantile_levels =0.10.50.9 # Quantile for probabilistic forecast id_column = \"id\" # Column identifying different time series timestamp_column = \"timestamp\" # Column with datetime information target = \"target\"# Column(s) with time series values to predict", "score": 0.7323053, "raw_content": null, "id": "0a43f8-01"}, {"url": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/fast-and-accurate-zero-shot-forecasting-with-chronos-bolt-and-autogluon", "title": "Fast and accurate zero-shot forecasting with Chronos-Bolt and AutoGluon | Artificial Intelligence", "content": "Chronos-Bolt is based on the T5 encoder-decoder architecture  and has been trained on nearly 100 billion time series observations. It chunks the historical time series context into patches of multiple observations, which are then input into the encoder. The decoder then uses these representations to directly generate quantile forecasts across multiple future steps—a method known as direct multi-step forecasting. This differs from the original Chronos models that rely on autoregressive decoding. [...] Foundation models (FMs) such as Chronos  have taken the idea of training a single model across multiple time series a significant step further. These models are pretrained on a vast corpus of real and synthetic time series data, covering diverse domains, frequencies, and history lengths. As a result, they enable zero-shot forecasting—delivering accurate predictions on unseen time series datasets. This lowers the entry barrier to forecasting and greatly simplifies forecasting pipelines by [...] ### About the Authors\n\nAbdul Fatir Ansari is a Senior Applied Scientist at Amazon Web Services, specializing in machine learning and forecasting, with a focus on foundation models for structured data, such as time series. He received his PhD from the National University of Singapore, where his research centered on deep generative models for images and time series.", "score": 0.5303587, "raw_content": null, "id": "193fd6-02"}, {"url": "https://galileo.ai/blog/amazon-chronos-ai-time-series-forecasting-guide", "title": "Amazon Chronos: Complete Guide to AI Time Series Forecasting", "content": "Think of a large language model, but instead of predicting the next word, it anticipates the next data point in your sales graph, sensor feed, or energy curve. That's Amazon Chronos—a transformer-based, pre-trained foundation model built specifically for time-series forecasting within the AWS ecosystem. [...] Think of a large language model, but instead of predicting the next word, it anticipates the next data point in your sales graph, sensor feed, or energy curve. That's Amazon Chronos—a transformer-based, pre-trained foundation model built specifically for time-series forecasting within the AWS ecosystem. [...] Think of a large language model, but instead of predicting the next word, it anticipates the next data point in your sales graph, sensor feed, or energy curve. That's Amazon Chronos—a transformer-based, pre-trained foundation model built specifically for time-series forecasting within the AWS ecosystem.", "score": 0.47606868, "raw_content": null, "id": "d81a66-03"}, {"url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/data-poem-launches-poem365-worlds-133000793.html", "title": "DATA POEM Launches POEM365, The World's First Large Causal AI Model (LCM)", "content": "tech companies have struggled to exceed. The large causal architecture also beats the benchmark datasets of all large AI time series models, including Times FM by Google, Chronos by Amazon, Moirai by Salesforce, TTM by IBM and the latest benchmark leader Toto by DATA DOG, across all time horizons and accuracy metrics.", "score": 0.22955288, "raw_content": null, "id": "cae39b-04"}], "response_time": 1.15, "request_id": "f3e624e6-44b0-496a-9222-3ca6bfd6082f"}{"query": "Lag-Llama Meta time series foundation model architecture parameters", "follow_up_questions": null, "answer": null, "images": [], "results": [{"url": "https://tsfm.ai/blog/lag-llama-open-source-tsfm", "title": "Lag-Llama: The Open-Source Time Series Foundation Model — TSFM.ai", "content": "Lag-Llama is notably compact. The published Hugging Face checkpoint is about 2.45M parameters, making it one of the smallest TSFMs in the current landscape. For comparison, Chronos-Large has 710M parameters, Moirai-Large has 311M, and TimesFM has 200M. Despite this size difference, Lag-Llama achieves competitive results on standard benchmarks, which speaks to the efficiency of its lag-based representation. [...] The explosion of foundation models in time series has produced a spectrum of architectural approaches: patching and binning in Chronos, masked encoding in Moirai, residual patching in TimesFM. Lag-Llama, introduced by Rasul et al. at ServiceNow Research in late 2023, takes a different path. It applies the decoder-only transformer architecture, directly inspired by Meta's LLaMA, to time series forecasting using lag features as input tokens. The result is a compact, probabilistic model that [...] Unlike models that produce point forecasts and rely on post-hoc methods for uncertainty, Lag-Llama is inherently probabilistic. The output at each timestep is not a single predicted value but the parameters of a Student-t distribution: location (mu), scale (sigma), and degrees of freedom (nu).", "score": 0.8184036, "raw_content": null, "id": "6d85af-00"}, {"url": "https://medium.com/@kdk199604/lag-llama-unifying-time-series-forecasting-with-foundation-model-principles-c6bf4e0dbb26", "title": "Lag-Llama: Unifying Time-Series Forecasting with Foundation Model Principles | by Dong-Keon Kim | Medium", "content": "Model size is another critical factor influencing zero-shot forecasting quality. The authors train Lag-Llama models with parameter counts ranging from 10³ to 3 × 10⁷. Figure 4 shows that on the Traffic dataset, models with more than 10⁶ parameters begin to outperform the best supervised baseline, with performance stabilizing in the range of 10⁶–10⁷ parameters. These findings highlight the importance of model capacity in capturing complex temporal dynamics and achieving strong generalization. [...] For probabilistic output, Lag-Llama employs a Student’s t-distribution as the predictive distribution. This choice provides robustness to heavy-tailed noise and allows the model to estimate uncertainty directly. The distribution head outputs three parameters at each time step: mean μ, scale σ, and degrees of freedom ν.\n\n## Get Dong-Keon Kim’s stories in your inbox\n\nJoin Medium for free to get updates from this writer. [...] The architecture of Lag-Llama is a Transformer decoder stack influenced by the LLaMA model, enhanced with RMSNorm normalization and Rotary Positional Encoding (RoPE). As depicted in Figure 2 of the paper, the model processes lag feature sequences of length C through a shared linear projection into the attention module’s hidden dimension. These sequences are passed through M masked Transformer decoder layers.", "score": 0.8051581, "raw_content": null, "id": "110fac-01"}, {"url": "https://andlukyane.com/blog/paper-review-lagllama", "title": "Paper Review: Lag-Llama: Towards Foundation Models for Probabilistic Time Series Forecasting | Andrey Lukyanenko", "content": "The final component of Lag-Llama is the distribution head, a layer that translates the model’s learned features into parameters of a specific probability distribution. In their experiments, the creators adopted a Student’s t-distribution, configuring the distribution head to output its three parameters: degrees of freedom, mean, and scale, with special adjustments to maintain the positivity of these parameters. [...] Lag-Llama is a new foundation model designed for univariate probabilistic time series forecasting, using a decoder-only transformer architecture with lags as covariates. It is pretrained on a diverse corpus of time series data from various domains, showcasing exceptional zero-shot generalization capabilities. When fine-tuned on small subsets of new datasets, Lag-Llama achieves superior performance, surpassing previous deep learning methods and setting new benchmarks in time series forecasting. [...] Lag-Llama uses a decoder-only transformer architecture, based on LLaMA, designed for univariate time series forecasting. The model processes sequences by first tokenizing them along with covariates into a sequence of tokens, which are then mapped to a hidden dimension suitable for the attention module. It incorporates pre-normalization techniques like RMSNorm and Rotary Positional Encoding to enhance its attention mechanism, aligning with the practices of the LLaMA architecture. The transformer", "score": 0.798077, "raw_content": null, "id": "4132a7-02"}, {"url": "https://github.com/time-series-foundation-models/lag-llama", "title": "GitHub - time-series-foundation-models/lag-llama: Lag-Llama: Towards Foundation Models for Probabilistic Time Series Forecasting · GitHub", "content": "We recommend tuning two important hyperparameters for each dataset that you finetune on: the context length (suggested values: $32$, $64$, $128$, $256$, $512$, $1024$) and the learning rate (suggested values: $10^{-2}$, $5 \\ 10^{-3}$, $10^{-3}$, $5 \\ 10^{-3}$, $1 \\ 10^{-4}$, $5 \\ 10^{-4}$).\n We also highly recommend using a validation split of your dataset to early stop your model, with an early stopping patience of 50 epochs.\n\n## Contact [...] Lag-Llama is a probabilistic forecasting model trained to output a probability distribution for each timestep to be predicted. For your own specific use-case, we would recommend benchmarking the zero-shot performance of the model on your data first, and then finetuning if necessary. As we show in our paper, Lag-Llama has strong zero-shot capabilities, but performs best when finetuned. The more data you finetune on, the better. For specific tips on applying on model zero-shot or on finetuning, [...] | Name | Name | Last commit message | Last commit date |\n ---  --- |\n| configs | configs |  |  |\n| data | data |  |  |\n| gluon\\_utils | gluon\\_utils |  |  |\n| images | images |  |  |\n| lag\\_llama | lag\\_llama |  |  |\n| scripts | scripts |  |  |\n| utils | utils |  |  |\n| .gitignore | .gitignore |  |  |\n| LICENSE | LICENSE |  |  |\n| MANIFEST.in | MANIFEST.in |  |  |\n| README.md | README.md |  |  |\n| VERSION | VERSION |  |  |\n| pyproject.toml | pyproject.toml |  |  |", "score": 0.79637206, "raw_content": null, "id": "913bff-03"}, {"url": "https://www.irina-lab.ai/blog/lag-llama", "title": "CERC-AAI Lab - Lag-LLaMA", "content": "## Lag-Llama is a general-purpose foundation model for univariate probabilistic time series forecasting based on a decoder-only transformer architecture that uses lags as covariates. Lag-Llama is pretrained on a large corpus of diverse time series data from several domains, and demonstrates strong zero-shot generalization capabilities compared to a wide range of forecasting models on downstream datasets across domains. Moreover, when fine-tuned on relatively small fractions of such previously [...] ### A LLaMA-like architecture for time series\n\n  \n\nLag-Llama is based on the recent decoder-only transformer-based LlaMA architecture which incorporates pre-layer normalization via the RMSNorm and adds Rotary Positional Encoding (RoPE) to each attention layer’s query and key representations.\n\n  \n\nAt inference time, given a time series of sufficient length, we construct a feature vector that is passed to the model to obtain the distribution of the next time point. [...] of such previously unseen datasets, Lag-Llama achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming prior deep learning approaches, emerging as the best general-purpose model on average. Lag-Llama serves as a strong contender to the current state-of-art in time series forecasting and paves the way for future advancements in foundation models tailored to time series data.", "score": 0.74251556, "raw_content": null, "id": "3f671c-04"}], "response_time": 1.13, "request_id": "ffce04b9-ffe9-4191-8ace-533a03a43208"}{"query": "TimesFM Google time series foundation model architecture parameters Moirai Salesforce", "follow_up_questions": null, "answer": null, "images": [], "results": [{"url": "https://mychen76.medium.com/time-series-foundation-models-for-forecasting-task-c9076cae9a84", "title": "Medium", "content": "--\n\nListen\n\nShare\n\nFrom late 2024 to early 2025, a wave of new time-series foundation models was released by major vendors like Amazon, Google, Salesforce, and others. These latest zero-shot foundational models for time series forecasting — including TimesFM (Google), Chronos (Amazon), Moirai (Salesforce), TimeGPT, Lag-LLama (ServiceNow) and Timer-XL (THUML) — which mark a significant leap forward in the field of time series forecasting models. [...] The architecture of these foundation models includes the following flavors:\n\nFor more information about Transformer architecture, please visit:", "score": 0.80442166, "raw_content": null, "id": "cc2074-00"}, {"url": "https://www.salesforce.com/blog/time-series-morai-moe", "title": "Moirai-MoE: Next-Gen Time Series Model | Salesforce", "content": "## Share article\n\nTL;DR: We propose Moirai-MoE, the first mixture-of-experts time series foundation model, achieving token-level model specialization in a data-driven manner. Extensive experiments on 39 datasets reveal that Moirai-MoE delivers up to 17% performance improvements over Moirai at the same level of model size and outperforms other time series foundation models, such as Chronos (from Amazon) and TimesFM (from Google) with up to 65 times fewer activated parameters. [...] When compared to all sizes of Moirai, Moirai-MoE-Small delivers a 3%–14% improvement in CRPS and an 8%–16% improvement in MASE. These improvements are remarkable, considering that Moirai-MoE-Small has only 11M activated parameters — 28 times fewer than Moirai-Large. [...] To excel in zero-shot forecasting, time series foundation models are pre-trained on massive data from a variety of sources. However, time series data is inherently heterogeneous, posing significant challenges for unified time series training. Existing approaches introduce some level of model specialization to account for the highly heterogeneous nature of time series data. For instance, Moirai employs multiple input/output projection layers, each tailored to handle time series at a specific", "score": 0.75355774, "raw_content": null, "id": "31ee24-01"}, {"url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/data-poem-launches-poem365-worlds-133000793.html", "title": "DATA POEM Launches POEM365, The World's First Large Causal AI Model (LCM)", "content": "tech companies have struggled to exceed. The large causal architecture also beats the benchmark datasets of all large AI time series models, including Times FM by Google, Chronos by Amazon, Moirai by Salesforce, TTM by IBM and the latest benchmark leader Toto by DATA DOG, across all time horizons and accuracy metrics.", "score": 0.6533265, "raw_content": null, "id": "6331b3-02"}, {"url": "https://www.datasciencewithmarco.com/blog/hands-on-with-moirai-a-foundation-forecasting-model-by-salesforce", "title": "Hands-On with Moirai: A Foundation Forecasting Model by Salesforce", "content": "In the code block above, notice that we specify the number of samples with the `num_samples` parameter. Since Moirai is a probabilistic model, it needs to draw many samples to generate a reasonable prediction interval. [...] Of course, this a Transformer-based model, which is the same architecture that powers Lag-Llama, Chronos, and most large language models (LLMs).\n\nThen, Moirai tries to be a universal forecaster, meaning that it can generate predictions of any time series. This is a challenging task, as time series are highly heterogeneous. [...] In May 2024, a new open-source foundation forecasting model was released: Moirai. In the paper Unified Training of Universal Time Series Forecasting Transformers, researchers from Salesforce propose a foundation model capable of probabilistic zero-shot forecasting, while also supporting exogenous features.", "score": 0.6437107, "raw_content": null, "id": "d57641-03"}, {"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kBu8Ko1b6rs", "title": "Zero shot forecasting. Time Series Models: MOIRAI-2, TimesFM-2.5, and Toto-2, Chronos, MOMENT", "content": "[8:35] Gotcha. So architecturally, times FM made a pretty big shift from time GPT1's encoder decoder setup. They went for a\n[8:44] decoder only attention model. That sounds a lot like the GPT architecture in NLP, doesn't it?\n[8:49] It's very analogous. Yeah. And that choice brings some key technical benefits like what? [...] [38:32] foundation models, it seems to have been effectively closed or at least significantly narrowed by the second wave. We've discussed models like Times FM, Moira RAI, and Toto.\n[38:43] Yeah, it feels like a real step change.\n[38:44] And they've done it not just by getting bigger, though the scale is certainly part of it, but by getting architecturally smarter, using patching [...] [6:37] You got it. The architecture was trying to be global, universal, but the task often demanded really specific local\n[6:44] precision. They demonstrated universality but sometimes at the cost of accuracy against a hyperfocused specialized model.\n[6:52] So the architecture was global but the task required local precision. The first generation was demonstrating universality but occasionally sacrificing accuracy against a hyperfocused opponent.", "score": 0.6342494, "raw_content": null, "id": "3df7cd-04"}], "response_time": 0.94, "request_id": "cb4fc6e1-03dc-4d38-bdb6-4cd410e1501f"}{"query": "TimesFM Google 200M parameters decoder-only architecture patching time series", "follow_up_questions": null, "answer": null, "images": [], "results": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2310.10688v2", "title": "A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting", "content": "## 7 Conclusion\n\nIn this paper, we presented TimesFM, a practical foundation model for forecasting whose zero-shot performance comes close to the accuracy of fully-supervised forecasting models on a diverse set of time series data. This model is trained on real-world and synthetic datasets comprising around 100B timepoints using a patched-decoder style attention architecture with around 200M parameters. [...] TimesFM. For our main 200M model we use 16 attention heads, 20 layers, a input patch length of 32 and output patch length of 128. The model dimension is set to 1280. We train with layer norm and a cosine decay learning rate schedule with peak learning rate of 5​e−45e-4. [...] models based on the number of tokens available in a training dataset. We perform a preliminary scaling study where we train three TimesFM models of sizes 17M, 70M and 200M parameters, using the same pretraining dataset and till the similar number of iterations.", "score": 0.82254606, "raw_content": null, "id": "824063-00"}, {"url": "https://aihorizonforecast.substack.com/p/timesfm-googles-foundation-model", "title": "TimesFM: Google's Foundation Model For Time-Series Forecasting", "content": "In other words:\n\nTimesFM is trained in decoder-only mode, similar to GPT models. The difference is in treating each token as a patch of time-points. Patching significantly improves inference speed and avoids constraining the model to a specific prediction length/horizon.\n\nAnd there you have it! We have outlined the architecture of TimesFM. [...] Remember, the choice of breaking the input into patches and allowing the output patches to be larger is the key design choice of TimesFM. If we didn’t have input patches (e.g., the model consumed the full input in a single step), TimesFM would essentially function as an encoder-decoder model, not decoder-only.\n\nTherefore, the authors measured how the performance and the size of input/output patches are related. The analysis is shown in Figure 6 and Figure 7: [...] Assume we have:\n\n a context-length of size L,\n and patches of size p,\n then we break the input into N = L/p patches. These are called input patches.\n We also have output patches of size h(horizon).\n By allowing `output_patch_size` > `input_patch_size`, authors found that TimesFM can learn to predict a horizon of any length faster and more accurately.\n\nFigure 1 shows the TimesFM architecture during training:\n\nFigure 1: TimesFM architecture overview (Source, annotated by author)", "score": 0.77792513, "raw_content": null, "id": "0225af-01"}, {"url": "https://research.google/blog/a-decoder-only-foundation-model-for-time-series-forecasting", "title": "A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting", "content": "## Conclusion\n\nWe train a decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting using a large pretraining corpus of 100B real world time-points, the majority of which was search interest time-series data derived from Google Trends and pageviews from Wikipedia. We show that even a relatively small 200M parameter pretrained model that uses our TimesFM architecture displays impressive zero-shot performance on a variety of public benchmarks from different domains and granularities. [...] To that end, in “A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting”, accepted at ICML 2024, we introduce TimesFM, a single forecasting model pre-trained on a large time-series corpus of 100 billion real world time-points. Compared to the latest large language models (LLMs), TimesFM is much smaller (200M parameters), yet we show that even at such scales, its zero-shot performance on a variety of unseen datasets of different domains and temporal granularities come close to the [...] Consider a time-series of length 512 time-points being used to train a TimesFM model with input patch length 32 and output patch length 128. During training, the model is simultaneously trained to use the first 32 time-points to forecast the next 128 time-points, the first 64 time-points to forecast time-points 65 to 192, the first 96 time-points to forecast time-points 97 to 224 and so on. During inference, suppose the model is given a new time-series of length 256 and tasked with forecasting", "score": 0.7742597, "raw_content": null, "id": "15e863-02"}, {"url": "https://huggingface.co/google/timesfm-2.5-200m-transformers", "title": "google/timesfm-2.5-200m-transformers · Hugging Face", "content": "TimesFM (Time Series Foundation Model) is a pretrained decoder-only model for time-series forecasting. This repository contains the Transformers port of the official TimesFM 2.5 PyTorch release.\n\nResources and Technical Documentation:\n\n## Model description\n\nThis model is converted from the official TimesFM 2.5 PyTorch checkpoint and integrated into `transformers` as `TimesFm2_5ModelForPrediction`.\n\n`transformers`\n`TimesFm2_5ModelForPrediction` [...] ## Paper for google/timesfm-2.5-200m-transformers\n\n#### A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting [...] ## Citation\n\n`@inproceedings{das2024a,\ntitle={A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting},\nauthor={Abhimanyu Das and Weihao Kong and Rajat Sen and Yichen Zhou},\nbooktitle={Forty-first International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2024},\nurl={\n}`\n\nFiles info\n\n## Model tree for google/timesfm-2.5-200m-transformers\n\n## Spaces using google/timesfm-2.5-200m-transformers 9\n\n## Collection including google/timesfm-2.5-200m-transformers\n\n#### TimesFM Release", "score": 0.6578248, "raw_content": null, "id": "6e29e3-03"}, {"url": "https://github.com/google-research/timesfm", "title": "GitHub - google-research/timesfm: TimesFM (Time Series Foundation Model) is a pretrained time-series foundation model developed by Google Research for time-series forecasting. · GitHub", "content": "Paper: A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting, ICML 2024.\n 🆕 TimesFM 3.0 Checkpoint: `google/timesfm-3.0-pytorch`. Checkpoints up to 2.5: TimesFM Hugging Face Collection.\n Google Reseach blog posts:\n  + TimesFM-3: A zero-shot foundation model for multivariate forecasting\n  + A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting\n TimesFM in Google 1P Products:\n  + BigQuery ML: Enterprise level SQL queries for scalability and reliability. [...] Comparing to TimesFM 2.0, this new 2.5 model:\n\n uses 200M parameters, down from 500M.\n supports up to 16k context length, up from 2048.\n supports continuous quantile forecast up to 1k horizon via an optional 30M quantile head.\n gets rid of the `frequency` indicator.\n has a couple of new forecasting flags.\n\nSince the Sept. 2025 launch, the following improvements have been completed for TimesFM 2.5: [...] + Google Sheets: For your daily spreadsheet.\n  + Vertex Model Garden: Dockerized endpoint for agentic calling.", "score": 0.63397753, "raw_content": null, "id": "7a0f69-04"}], "response_time": 1.93, "request_id": "5d68291d-49a1-40f3-9df4-f1ee86a271a7"}{"query": "Moirai Salesforce 311M parameters architecture masked encoder time series foundation model", "follow_up_questions": null, "answer": null, "images": [], "results": [{"url": "https://towardsdatascience.com/moirai-salesforces-foundation-model-for-time-series-forecasting-4eff6c34093d", "title": "MOIRAI: Salesforce's Foundation Model for Time-Series Forecasting | Towards Data Science", "content": "TimesFM is a decoder-style Transformer, while MOIRAI mostly resembles an encoder-based architecture.\n MOIRAI uses multiple patches to accommodate different frequencies, while TimesFM supports one type of patching.\n The largest MOIRAI has more parameters (311M) compared to TimesFM(200M). On the other hand, TimesFM seems to be trained on more data compared to TimesFM (100B vs 27B). [...] ## Training\n\nAn important contribution of this paper is the LOTSA dataset, which consists of 27B observations and spans 9 domains (Figure 6):\n\nFigure 6: Characteristics of the LOTSA dataset, by frequency and domain (Source)\n\nFigure 6: Characteristics of the LOTSA dataset, by frequency and domain (Source)\n\nAlso, the authors trained 3 MOIRAI models, of sizes 14M, 91M, and 311M parameters. Their characteristics are shown in Figure 7: [...] CriticismMOIRAI does not provide any details regarding how the model scales in terms of parameter size, data, and training time. Remember, the potential of a large Transformer-based model depends on its ability to leverage scaling laws.\n\nTimesFM does not provide specific details either - but at least its authors show it becomes better with more data.\n\n## Closing Remarks\n\nMOIRAI is a foundation time-series model that will revolutionize time-series forecasting.", "score": 0.8014549, "raw_content": null, "id": "ba50f8-00"}, {"url": "https://www.salesforce.com/blog/moirai", "title": "Moirai: A Time Series Foundation Model for Universal Forecasting", "content": "Illustrated in Figure 2, Moirai follows a (non-overlapping) patch-based approach to modeling time series with a masked encoder architecture. One of our proposed modifications to extend the architecture to the any-variate setting is to “flatten” multivariate time series, considering all variates as a single sequence. Patches are subsequently projected into vector representations via a multi-patch size input projection layer. The [mask] signifies a learnable embedding that replaces patches [...] ### Results\n\nWe train Moirai in 3 sizes – small/base/large with 14m/91m/311m parameters! On in-distribution evaluations using the Monash Time Series Forecasting Benchmark, Moirai displays phenomenal performance, beating all baselines. [...] TL;DR: Moirai is a cutting-edge time series foundation model, offering universal forecasting capabilities. It stands out as a versatile time series forecasting model capable of addressing diverse forecasting tasks across multiple domains, frequencies, and variables in a zero-shot manner.  To achieve this, Moirai tackles four major challenges: (i) construction of a LOTSA, a large-scale and diverse time series dataset, comprising 27 billion observations spanning nine distinct domains, (ii)", "score": 0.79215926, "raw_content": null, "id": "bcf7d3-01"}, {"url": "https://medium.com/@kdk199604/moirai-building-a-universal-forecaster-for-time-series-data-c959b3b35174", "title": "MOIRAI: Building a Universal Forecaster for Time Series Data | by Dong-Keon Kim | Medium", "content": "MOIRAI follows a non-overlapping patch-based masked encoder architecture. Unlike decoder-only autoregressive models, the model masks the forecast horizon and predicts the distribution of future values from encoded context and covariate information. The Transformer backbone adopts several modern design choices from large language models, including pre-normalization, RMSNorm, query-key normalization, SwiGLU feed-forward layers, and bias-free linear layers. These choices make the architecture more [...] MOIRAI presents a strong step toward universal time series forecasting. The paper introduces a masked encoder Transformer that can handle multiple frequencies, arbitrary numbers of variates, and flexible predictive distributions. Its unified training on LOTSA enables zero-shot forecasting performance that is competitive with, and sometimes superior to, full-shot models trained on target datasets. The work is significant because it shifts time series forecasting toward the foundation model [...] The model is trained in three sizes: MOIRAI Small with 14M parameters, MOIRAI Base with 91M parameters, and MOIRAI Large with 311M parameters. Small is trained for 100K steps, while Base and Large are trained for 1M steps with batch size 256. The optimizer is AdamW with learning rate 10⁻³, weight decay 10⁻¹, β₁ = 0.9, and β₂ = 0.98. The authors also use sequence packing to reduce padding waste caused by variable context, horizon, and variate lengths. The details of the MOIRAI and its variants", "score": 0.71040785, "raw_content": null, "id": "c52f50-02"}, {"url": "https://www.datasciencewithmarco.com/blog/hands-on-with-moirai-a-foundation-forecasting-model-by-salesforce", "title": "Hands-On with Moirai: A Foundation Forecasting Model by Salesforce", "content": "Quickly, we see that the series are first patched. In the figure above, Variate 0 and Variate 1 are target series (series to be forecasted) while Variate 2 contains future values available at the time of prediction. This is why Variate 0 and 1 have a shaded region due to a mask, while Variate 2 is seen completely.\n\nThe patched series are then sent through the embedding layer and continue to the Transformer with self-attention. Note that in Moirai, it is an encoder-only Transformer. [...] Learn the latest time series forecasting techniques with my free time series cheat sheet in Python! Get the implementation of statistical and deep learning techniques, all in Python and TensorFlow!\n\nLet’s get started!\n\n### Discover Moirai\n\nThe model Moirai stands for Masked Encoder-based Universal Time Series Forecasting Transformer. Admittedly, this is not the most intuitive acronym for such a lengthy name, but it does tell us what Moirai aims to achieve. [...] Of course, this a Transformer-based model, which is the same architecture that powers Lag-Llama, Chronos, and most large language models (LLMs).\n\nThen, Moirai tries to be a universal forecaster, meaning that it can generate predictions of any time series. This is a challenging task, as time series are highly heterogeneous.", "score": 0.69795954, "raw_content": null, "id": "0d98da-03"}, {"url": "https://aihorizonforecast.substack.com/p/moirai-moe-upgrading-moirai-with", "title": "MOIRAI-MOE : Upgrading MOIRAI with Mixture-of-Experts for Enhanced Time Series Forecasting", "content": "MOIRAI-MOE is a Decoder-only, Foundation time-series model using Mixture-of-Experts to perform generalizable, frequency-invariant forecasting with fewer parameters.\n\nA comparison between the two models (original MOIRAI vs. MOIRAI-MOE) is shown in Figure 1:\n\nFigure 1: MOIRAI vs MOIRAI-MOE architecture (Source)\n\nLet’s break down these differences:\n\n1. MOIRAI-MOE is now a Decoder-only modelMOIRAI-MOE departs from MOIRAI’s original masked encoder architecture, adopting a decoder-only setup.", "score": 0.6780931, "raw_content": null, "id": "f8a74b-04"}], "response_time": 1.05, "request_id": "a00aa3af-a879-498e-bf85-da9ce294e54e"}{"message": "Successfully created file at E:\\agent_dev\\multi-agent\\experiments\\costprobe\\sb\\tmp\\public_tsfm_architecture_comparison.md"}[Most recent upstream result] ## Step 2/6 完成：公开金融时序基础模型架构检索

### 核心发现

已检索并整理 6 个主流公开时序基础模型的架构细节：

| 模型 | 机构 | 架构 | 参数量 | 训练数据 | 开源 |
|------|------|------|--------|---------|------|
| **TimeGPT** | Nixtla | Encoder-Decoder Transformer | 未公开 | 100B+ 数据点 | ❌ API only |
| **Chronos** | Amazon | T5 Encoder-Decoder / Encoder-only | 8M–710M | ~100B 观测 | ✅ |
| **Lag-Llama** | ServiceNow | Decoder-only（LLaMA 风格） | 2.45M（公开） | 多领域语料 | ✅ |
| **TimesFM** | Google | Decoder-only（patched） | 200M（2.5 版） | ~100B 数据点 | ✅ |
| **Moirai** | Salesforce | Masked Encoder（patch-based） | 14M/91M/311M | 27B（LOTSA） | ✅ |
| **Moirai-MoE** | Salesforce | Decoder-only + MoE | 11M 激活 | 同 Moirai | ✅ |

### 关键架构趋势

1. **从 Encoder-Decoder 到 Decoder-only**：TimesFM、Moirai-MoE 采用 decoder-only，推理更快
2. **Patch 化输入**：TimesFM（输入 32/输出 128）、Chronos-Bolt、Moirai 均使用 patch 减少 token 数
3. **概率预测成为标配**：Lag-Llama（Student-t）、Chronos（分位数）、Moirai（灵活分布）
4. **MoE 出现**：Moirai-MoE 用 11M 激活参数达到 Moirai-Large 性能
5. **模型规模缩小**：TimesFM 2.0（500M）→ 2.5（200M），趋势是更高效而非更大

### 与 XTX 的类别级对比

- **公开 TSFM**：通用预测器，2M–710M 参数，毫秒–秒级推理，需微调适配金融
- **XTX（推测）**：专用交易引擎，数十亿+ 参数（20k+ GPU 训练），微秒级推理，原生金融多变量

**关键结论**：XTX 的护城河不在架构创新，而在**数据规模 + 算力 + 实时性 + 闭环迭代**。公开 TSFM 是"通用预测器"，XTX 是"专用交易引擎"。

